// The pieces every x86 encoding is built from, written once here instead of
// once per width and once per register range.
//
// An `if` over the operands is a shape: the encoder picks the bit map whose
// guard holds. That is how one instruction spells both the REX and the REX-free
// form of the same operation, which the model used to carry as twin
// instructions plus a post-register-allocation pass that swapped one for the
// other.

// The 0100 WRXB prefix. Present when the operation needs the wide bit (`w`), or
// a register needs its fourth bit (`reg[3]`/`rm[3]`), or the caller says it is
// needed anyway (`needed`) — which is how an 8-bit form reaches spl/bpl/sil/dil,
// whose names exist only behind a REX byte.
fn rex(w, reg, rm, needed) {
    if w | reg[3] | rm[3] | needed {
        (0b0100, w, reg[3], 0b0, rm[3])
    } else {
        ()
    }
}

// ModR/M with both operands in registers: mod=11, then the two low register
// numbers. `reg` is the /r field, `rm` the r/m field.
fn modrm_reg(reg, rm) {
    (0b11, reg[2..0], rm[2..0])
}

// Whether an 8-bit operand names a register only a REX byte can reach: spl,
// bpl, sil and dil are numbers 4..7, which without REX name ah, ch, dh and bh.
fn needs_rex8(a, b) {
    (a ~>= 0b0100) | (b ~>= 0b0100)
}
